    "narrative": "Our most potent offensive threats today are anchored by **Gunnar Henderson** (65.74) and **Vladimir Guerrero Jr.** (63.15), both of whom overcome moderate SP_xERA matchups through high-floor production and favorable park factors. **Gunnar Henderson** benefits from a substantial +16% park adjustment, making him our highest-scoring asset despite a tricky matchup against Shane McClanahan. Meanwhile, **Coby Mayo** (61.44) offers a surprising high-upside play at the utility spot; while batting lower in the order usually creates a drag, he is capitalizing on the same favorable park dynamics as **Gunnar Henderson**, positioning him as a stealthy producer for our Roto categories today.\n\nThe most difficult bench decision involved choosing between **Jackson Merrill** (59.11) and the trio of **Heliot Ramos**, **Nasim Nunez**, and **Drake Baldwin**. **Jackson Merrill** secured his spot despite a minor penalty for the opponent\u2019s elite pick-off ability, as his dynamic platoon advantage was too significant to ignore in our quest for stolen base efficiency. **Heliot Ramos** (45.34) was the closest exclusion; he offered a slight boost for his stolen base potential against a slow-delivering pitcher, but ultimately lacked the overall efficiency score to displace our starting outfielders. We remain cautious regarding **Liam Hicks**, **Sam Antonacci**, **Gunnar Henderson**, **Miguel Vargas**, **Luis Robert Jr.**, **Andy Pages**, **Jackson Merrill**, **Wade Meckler**, **Kyle Tucker**, and **Coby Mayo** as their scores rely on assumed lineup positions; any downward shift in their expected batting order could dampen their overall value, particularly for speed-reliant players like **Wade Meckler** and **Sam Antonacci**. While **Jordan Westburg** and **Jacob Wilson** remain sidelined on the IL, we continue to monitor the status of **Josh Jung**, **Dominic Smith**, **Curtis Mead**, **Luis Lara**, and **Tommy White** to ensure optimal coverage across all five categories.",
